WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.330
Microscopes are an essential tool for
biologists to see the tiny things they

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.600
study. But no matter how much you zoom
in with a microscope, some things are

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.060
just really hard to see. This is partly
because when you look at something

4
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:17.060
under a microscope, a lot of things are
pretty much the same color. This is

5
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.570
where fluorescence microscopy comes in.
More than 50 years ago, scientists

6
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:25.080
found a special protein in some
jellyfish that make them glow under

7
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:30.140
ultraviolet light. This protein, named
green fluorescent protein, or GFP,

8
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.320
changes shape when hit by the right
color of light. This change in

9
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.390
structure makes GFP fluoresce. It
absorbs ultraviolet light and shines

10
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.840
out green light. With this discovery,
the way scientists use microscopy was

11
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.560
changed forever. How? Well, you might
be familiar with the traditional light

12
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.700
microscope. You've got your sample on a
stage, a bumble bee in this case, and a

13
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.600
lamp sends white light up through the
stage to your eye, with some lenses for

14
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.800
magnification in between. But now we
want to specifically look at the brain

15
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.520
of this bumble bee. With light
microscopy, we can't really tell the

16
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.010
difference between different
structures. So let's take a different

17
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:14.230
bumble bee, one where we've labeled the
brain with GFP. Under white light, it

18
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.040
doesn't look like much. But now we add
a filter that only lets the right

19
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.050
wavelength, or color, of light through.
GFP absorbs this color of light and

20
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:27.380
glows. To make the picture as clear as
possible, we add one more filter so

21
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.410
only the green light reaches our eyes,
and now we can see the bumble bee brain

22
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.640
as clear as day. Since the discovery of
GFP, a whole rainbow of different

23
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.530
fluorescent proteins have been
engineered: red, orange, yellow, blue

24
00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.490
and more. By using multiple fluorescent
proteins to color different parts of a

25
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:47.440
sample, we can very clearly see
whatever it is we might be looking for.

